WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.460
VIC IGLESIAS: So over
the course of the day,

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.070
you've seen a lot of
technologies presented and ways

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.320
in which you can optimize
for operators and developers

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.250
and things like that.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.792
What we want to do
in this session is

00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:11.670
show you something you
could build end-to-end

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.320
with these technologies
and how you would use them,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.270
kind of give you the
use case for how these

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.120
might be stitched together.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.750
So my name is Vic Iglesias.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.060
I'm a Cloud Solutions Architect.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.160
The other two are
Ameer and Steve.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.320
And they are the
brave folks who are

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.798
going to do the live
demo portion of what

00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:28.590
I'll be explaining.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.440
So I get the easy part here
and they're the real heroes.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.030
So I'll first talk about why
you should build a platform.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:35.447
Then we'll talk
about the platform

00:00:35.447 --> 00:00:38.640
that we're demoing today
and what that looks like.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.970
Ameer will demo the
onboarding of an application

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:42.840
to the platform.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.220
And then Steve will talk about
how we roll out to production.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.210
So why should you
build a platform?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.040
We'll start this
conversation by talking

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.270
about what is a platform?

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.060
And really, it's this
idea of an abstraction

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:56.710
between your developers
and operators.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.570
It's the simplest
way I can put it.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.910
You want your developers
to have one single way

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.052
that they know how to
iterate on their services,

00:01:03.052 --> 00:01:04.510
and that they're
really good at it.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.760
But also, as operators,
you want to be

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.602
able to iterate on the
underlying infrastructure.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.560
You don't want to be
stuck on whatever decision

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.352
you made five years
ago for infrastructure.

00:01:13.352 --> 00:01:15.268
You want to be able to
iterate on those pieces

00:01:15.268 --> 00:01:17.440
while maintaining your
developers' productivity.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.360
That's a huge, huge task.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:20.860
So what we do is
we build platforms.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:22.443
We build these
abstractions that allow

00:01:22.443 --> 00:01:24.670
developers to do their
things and stay consistent.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.443
You might have 100
times more developers

00:01:28.443 --> 00:01:29.360
than you do operators.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:30.850
So you want to make sure
that those folks are

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.810
able to iterate.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.940
And how many folks
in here actually,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.530
like, their company
ships software

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.750
and then makes money from it?

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:38.250
Yeah.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.042
A lot of us, right?

00:01:39.042 --> 00:01:39.970
You have to do that.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:44.230
How many folks have to have
software to run your business?

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.820
The rest of them, right?

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:47.800
We need to maintain the
developer productivity

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:51.490
and keep high reliability as we
work through these challenges.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.230
When you look under
the covers, things

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.300
get pretty complicated
a lot of the time.

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.722
You started in your data center.

00:01:56.722 --> 00:01:57.430
Things were fine.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.440
You maybe had just dev and prod.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:00.940
People were just
SSH-ing to machines

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.220
and throwing JARs everywhere
from the very beginning.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.450
That got a little cagey.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.010
So you added a staging
logical environment,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.919
so you could test
out your changes.

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:11.080
Then from there, you
added a cloud provider.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.490
Maybe it was GCP, who knows?

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:15.250
And you started to
complicate things,

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.900
now having two physical
locations where

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.540
things are located also
having those many logical

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.320
environments.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.840
So things get complex under
the hood for these operators.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.700
And we want to make that easier.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.680
Some of the complications
you end up with here

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.040
are inconsistent ways that
people deploy, or think

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.170
about CI, or run their builds.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.990
And all of that stuff means that
you aren't sharing your best

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.400
practices with each other.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.330
All of the innovation
that you're

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.747
seeing in one part
of your company

00:02:38.747 --> 00:02:41.362
is not being leveraged
in the others.

00:02:41.362 --> 00:02:42.820
Another thing that
you start to see

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.225
is that those logical
environments don't actually

00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:46.600
mean what you
thought they meant.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:48.820
You thought you had a
staging environment that was

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:50.653
staged to be, like, production.

00:02:50.653 --> 00:02:52.570
But at the end of the
day, it's so far from it

00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:53.890
that you're not
even learning things

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.723
that would hit production,
and you're seeing

00:02:55.723 --> 00:02:57.340
your bugs hit prod anyway.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.350
Obviously, you can't
test for everything,

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:02.450
but that's the goal of having
those logical environments.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.230
The next thing is you
start figuring out

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.850
that it's hard to
even get people

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:08.350
the right access
to the right things

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:09.753
to be able to get
their job done.

00:03:09.753 --> 00:03:11.920
You want a standard way to
do those kinds of things,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.770
including configuring which
apps can connect to each other.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.770
Dan showed a great demo of
Istio on the service mesh,

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.280
and things that we can actually
use to connect services

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.770
in a secure and reliable way.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.390
The last one is mostly
for operators here,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.230
where each team does
ops differently.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:30.925
And so when an
outage hits, you have

00:03:30.925 --> 00:03:33.050
to figure out what their
best practices for logging

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:34.270
and monitoring and
all that stuff are,

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.060
and where their systems are, and
how they do their actual logs,

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.650
and whether they're JSON or not.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.540
You start to see a
lot of this stuff

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.535
when you're not
building platforms

00:03:42.535 --> 00:03:45.970
to operate-- for
developers and operators

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.860
to have an abstraction.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.970
So then you say, Vic,
we've had platforms.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.550
We've had things like
App Engine, things

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.980
like Cloud Foundry,
hosted platforms,

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.690
platforms as a service.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.750
In 2008, Google
launched App Engine.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.310
And this helped a
lot of companies

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.620
not think about all the
operational aspects.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.570
They just got to
scale to millions

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.210
of requests per second for free
with just a little bit of code.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.720
Turns out, this seemed very
opinionated to enterprises.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.180
It was too hard for them to
make a leap into the platform

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.360
as a service world.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.079
So we started to see
platforms, like infrastructure

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.747
as a service, like
Compute Engine in 2012,

00:04:21.747 --> 00:04:24.080
which was launched, which
gave you the most flexibility.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.040
You just run a VM and
put software on it.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.360
It's going to be easy.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.910
Turns out, that was
kind of hard as well.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.570
In 2015, we launched
Kubernetes Engine.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.550
And that was kind of a
happy medium for folks.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.390
It was enough of an
infrastructure abstraction.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:40.050
It turns out, it can run
kind of anywhere now.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.580
And this is now a
new building block

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.220
that folks are building their
kind of bespoke platforms

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.290
on top of.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.720
And what you get here is a
nice infrastructure abstraction

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.490
and then the ability
to add all the pieces

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.600
that you need in between.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.360
And there may be some bespoke
choices in your company,

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.500
like you chose a particular
identity provider or a secrets

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.480
management tool that
you need to integrate,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.790
and Kubernetes gives you
a lot of the flexibility

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:03.750
to make those choices.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.730
How many folks know this
gentleman, Kelsey Hightower?

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:09.660
He's awesome.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:12.360
We pray to him for
Ameer and Steve's sake

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.270
that the demo goes well.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.200
Kelsey said that
"Kubernetes is a platform

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.770
for building platforms."

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:19.260
It's not your endgame.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.610
It's a better place to start.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.068
And that's kind of
what we're going

00:05:22.068 --> 00:05:23.940
to do throughout this
talk and the demo.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.850
So how do you build
a platform on Anthos?

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.390
First thing to look
at is, what are

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.375
the layers of your
stack when you're

00:05:30.375 --> 00:05:31.500
talking about applications?

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.270
You have infrastructure
layers, which

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.440
is your networking,
your compute, storage,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.670
things like that.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.160
Anthos provides a way
to abstract that away.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.330
And so above that, you create
kind of platform tools or sets

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.830
of tools that make
up your platform.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:48.465
You might have a source code
management system, a CI system,

00:05:48.465 --> 00:05:51.090
and a continuous delivery system
that you build on top in order

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:55.830
to give your developers that
git commit to do the right thing

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.960
experience.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.550
So why would you build
on top of Anthos?

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:01.578
You get a central--

00:06:01.578 --> 00:06:03.120
centrally manage
your infrastructure.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.720
We had Anthos Config Management
as an example of that.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.790
You get a single API
that you know you

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:10.500
can use to deploy workloads.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.750
You have an interface, which
is like the Docker image.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.125
And then you know
from there, you

00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:17.820
can go to any of your locations
or environments and deploy.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.172
And it allows you to then
share those methodologies

00:06:20.172 --> 00:06:22.380
across your teams, or
centralize those methodologies,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:23.700
and then extend them.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.110
And as I said previously, you
can run Kubernetes and GKE

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:30.180
now anywhere, whether it
be on-prem or in the cloud.

00:06:33.362 --> 00:06:34.820
So as we're building
our platforms,

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.890
we're thinking about
three main personas--

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.860
the developers, the
security and policy folks,

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.240
who are trying to
figure out what

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.898
can't be done in the
platform, and our operators,

00:06:43.898 --> 00:06:46.190
who are just trying to keep
the lights on and make sure

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.832
that things are
running smoothly.

00:06:48.832 --> 00:06:50.290
In order build a
platform, we think

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.660
you need to have one of
each of these components.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.160
I talked about a lot of these.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:55.960
Dan talked about
app observability,

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.630
which we can introduce with
Istio and Anthos Service Mesh.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.750
We need a way to
manage our source code,

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.900
a way to manage
our configuration,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:06.280
CI/CD methodologies, a
way to manage our policy,

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.180
and then ways to
run our containers.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.130
So in this talk,
in the demo, we'll

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.920
go through what it's
like to build this out,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:15.840
choosing one of each
of these components,

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.257
and then stitching
it all together

00:07:17.257 --> 00:07:18.605
and showing it to you folks.

00:07:18.605 --> 00:07:19.980
As we've gone
through this, we've

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.930
seen that there's
really three interfaces

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.090
that these components use
to talk to each other.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.250
We have Git repositories,
so we can push and pull

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.540
code, source code.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.970
Sometimes that's a
Kubernetes manifest.

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:33.090
We can push and pull Docker
images from a repository.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.100
That's our deployable artifact.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.320
And then we configure
that deployable artifact

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.710
using Kubernetes manifest
that we deploy to clusters.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.520
The way that we're using
Anthos Configuration Management

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.790
in this example is by
using it to sync policy

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.720
and structure of namespaces
across our clusters.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.800
So we store all of
that configuration

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:55.780
in a centralized Git
repo, and then it

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:59.250
gets applied to all
of our clusters.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.100
And what we end up with in
each of these namespaces

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.530
that we create for
applications is one service

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.090
account that that
application uses

00:08:06.090 --> 00:08:08.400
to deploy within that cluster.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.080
It has a CI runner.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:11.770
So in our case,
we're using GitLab,

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:14.100
so we have a GitLab CI runner
running in that namespace

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:15.960
just for that application.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.200
And then that deploys
with that service

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.515
account all the resources
that the application requires,

00:08:21.515 --> 00:08:23.640
whether that's deployments
or services or ingresses

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.175
or anything like that.

00:08:25.175 --> 00:08:26.550
And then these
are all replicated

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.210
across each of our clusters.

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.160
So as we add a cluster
to an environment,

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:32.733
it gets the same exact
setup as the other clusters

00:08:32.733 --> 00:08:33.400
that's in there.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:35.215
It's a nice way to
centralize that.

00:08:35.215 --> 00:08:36.840
The other thing we're
going to be doing

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.370
is deployment with GitOps.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:41.010
So this has been an idea
that's been around for a while

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:43.543
and now has a name to it--
the GitOps methodology.

00:08:43.543 --> 00:08:44.960
And what that means
is we're going

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.430
to have our application
configuration living inside

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:49.920
of an app repo, and we're
going to have our Kubernetes

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.710
config in there, and then we're
going to hydrate that manifest.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.220
We're going to render that
manifest into a fully-fledged

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.882
Kubernetes manifest that can
be applied into a cluster.

00:08:58.882 --> 00:09:00.840
But before doing that,
we're going to commit it

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.150
to a Git repository.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:06.810
And that gives us a way to
track what was deployed and when

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.000
and what the diffs were
between those commits.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.730
So in this example,
we're going to have

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.730
that environment repo have
two branches, one staging

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.610
branch, which maps to what's
in the staging clusters,

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:20.280
and then one master branch,
which maps to what's

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.080
in production.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.780
As far as managing our
configuration, our application

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.363
configuration, we're
going to be using

00:09:27.363 --> 00:09:29.530
Kustomize, which
is a way to manage

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:30.880
your Kubernetes manifest.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.410
Rather than templating
them, you're

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:35.860
going to be able to create
a base set of manifests,

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:39.050
and then apply patches to
them for various use cases.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:40.660
Another thing that
Kustomize can do

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.470
is apply labels and annotations
across a set of resources

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.510
and/or add a prefix.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.760
So a big use case
for adding a prefix

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:53.175
is I want to prefix everything
with the environment name.

00:09:53.175 --> 00:09:54.550
So you have a base
that just says

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.130
what your resources are,
and then in Kustomize,

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:59.440
you can apply prod
dash or staging

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.740
dash to all the resources.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.560
So when you see them
in your dashboards

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.150
and in your Kubernetes
CLI, you can

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.338
tell which environment
you're in really easily

00:10:06.338 --> 00:10:08.380
without having to go into
each of those resources

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:09.338
and change it yourself.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:15.960
And one of the key methodologies
that Kustomize uses

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.210
is to have these patches
on top of a base.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.330
The other place that we see
this is in Docker images.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:25.680
You have in Docker, from
Ubuntu 16.04 or 18.04,

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.940
that's the base image.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:28.440
Then you run commands
on top of that

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.570
and make little tweaks
to the filesystem.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.090
We have a very similar
approach with Kustomize.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:34.970
So you have a set
of base manifests.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:37.350
And then from there, you
add overlays or patches

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.360
on top of that that
sprinkle in the changes

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.670
that you want without
changing that original base.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.920
That base might be used for
a completely different use

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.450
case in another
place, and you just

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:49.980
do what you need in order
to transform that for yours.

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:52.380
One concrete way you
could see this is I

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.080
have a base set of
Kubernetes manifests

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.010
that I call my application.

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:58.800
And then for each
of my environments,

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:02.010
I have environment-specific
patches

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.340
that I apply to each of
those before I finally

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.240
hydrate those and submit
them into my clusters.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.380
That's a very basic
use case for Kustomize.

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:15.060
So now on the demo, we'll
talk through a little bit

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:17.340
of the cloud architecture.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:19.270
We'll have five
clusters in this demo.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.338
We'll have one for dev.

00:11:20.338 --> 00:11:21.630
That is where they can iterate.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.213
They have some
namespaces that they're

00:11:23.213 --> 00:11:25.530
locked into in that cluster.

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.960
They iterate with something
like Skaffold or Cloud Run

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.240
on their own, and then
they commit some code.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:31.890
Oops.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:36.678
We need the slides back and
power up here, it looks like.

00:11:36.678 --> 00:11:38.220
So you have a tools
cluster in there.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.345
We have our CI/CD tools.

00:11:39.345 --> 00:11:41.220
All those platform pieces
that I talked about

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.158
in that layered
infrastructure diagram

00:11:43.158 --> 00:11:44.700
are going to be in
the tools cluster.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.200
We're using GitLab for this.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.018
And so the developers are going
to commit and merge in GitLab,

00:11:49.018 --> 00:11:51.060
and then that's going to
make its way to staging,

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.070
and then finally, to
production clusters.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:57.150
Here we have one staging cluster
and two production clusters.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.970
And I'll talk a little bit
about how the code repos work.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:03.420
Again, we're going to have
two main repos-- the code repo

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:04.980
and the environment repo.

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:08.130
When a developer commits
to a feature branch,

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.080
they're iterating on
their local environment.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.330
Then eventually, they're
going to make a merge request

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.270
to the master branch
of the CI repo.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.010
Then from there,
that's automatically

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:20.280
going to be committed to the
staging repo of-- or the, yeah,

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.370
staging branch of
the environment repo

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:24.760
and added to the
staging environment.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:27.330
So now everything that's in
master of the developer's repo

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.280
is ready to go in staging.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.820
Now, if we want to get a change
from staging to production,

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.200
we merge in the environment
repo from staging to master,

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.860
and that does a CI run or a
CD run that pushes directly

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.570
into the production clusters.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:46.300
So with that, we'll
kick it over to Ameer,

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:47.590
who will take us through it.

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:48.778
AMEER ABBAS: Sounds good.

00:12:48.778 --> 00:12:49.653
Can you guys hear me?

00:12:49.653 --> 00:12:50.236
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:12:50.236 --> 00:12:51.195
AMEER ABBAS: All right.

00:12:51.195 --> 00:12:52.855
Everybody clear on
what Vic just said?

00:12:52.855 --> 00:12:53.230
VIC IGLESIAS: Ha.

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:54.410
AMEER ABBAS: Do we
