00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.670
And I will be your operator
for the rest of the 10 minutes

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:10.780
or so that we have.

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:12.670
Let's all put on
our operators hats.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:15.070
And let's assume that our
developer just walked up to us

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:18.192
and said, hey, I have this
new Go app I'd like to deploy.

00:13:18.192 --> 00:13:19.150
Hi, Myles, How are you?

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:19.942
MYLES: [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:19.942 --> 00:13:21.727
AMEER ABBAS: Awesome.

00:13:21.727 --> 00:13:22.810
And then so what do we do?

00:13:22.810 --> 00:13:25.480
So like any other
platform as a service,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.850
we'd start with the CLI,
which is what you see up here.

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.340
We have this
handy-dandy CLI tool

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:33.310
that we are going to use to
add a new application called

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:37.220
hello-kubecon-1 into
our CI/CD platform.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:39.206
Maybe.

00:13:39.206 --> 00:13:41.581
All right, let me just make
sure I'm in the right folder.

00:13:41.581 --> 00:13:43.940
Huh?

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.020
Log out.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:46.246
Is that good?

00:13:46.246 --> 00:13:47.780
No, one more.

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:48.290
One more.

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.670
All right.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:51.808
CLI is a good way of
adding an application.

00:13:51.808 --> 00:13:52.850
It's nice and consistent.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:53.950
Everybody gets
treated the same way.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:55.370
You don't make any
kind of human mistakes.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:57.010
But in this
particular case, we're

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.412
using anthos-platform-cli.

00:13:58.412 --> 00:13:59.870
The name of our
application is just

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:01.615
going to be hello-kubecon-2019.

00:14:01.615 --> 00:14:03.740
We give it our CI/CD's
hostname, which in this case

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:07.470
is GitLab, the password,
and then the template.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.160
So again, template gives you
kind of that boilerplate code

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:11.750
kind of already made up.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:14.240
So what's happening
in the background,

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.737
I'll just quickly explain, is in
GitLab, it's creating a group.

00:14:17.737 --> 00:14:19.820
And a group is nothing
more than just a collection

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.718
of repositories, just like
an organization on GitHub.

00:14:22.718 --> 00:14:25.010
And in this case, it's creating
those two repositories.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:27.437
One is your application
code repositories.

00:14:27.437 --> 00:14:29.270
That's what you hand
off to your developers.

00:14:29.270 --> 00:14:31.560
That's where they iterate
on their applications.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.950
And then the other one is
your operator's repository

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:35.810
or your environment
repositories.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.790
That's where all of
the Kubernetes resource

00:14:37.790 --> 00:14:40.790
manifests reside for all
environments, so for dev,

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:41.833
stage, and prod.

00:14:41.833 --> 00:14:43.250
Keeping those two
separate kind of

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:45.710
keeps the CI of the
developer separated

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:47.390
from the CD of the operator.

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:48.752
It's a nice way to do that.

00:14:48.752 --> 00:14:50.210
Another thing that
Vic mentioned is

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:53.300
that every application gets
deployed in its own namespace

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:54.960
on every single cluster.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.200
And as part of that
namespace, a GitLab runner,

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.570
which is just a deployment,
is deployed inside

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.110
of that namespace.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.240
Now, there's a couple of
schools of thought here.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.560
One is, how do we push these
manifests down to the clusters?

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:07.860
So there's two ways to do that.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:11.480
We can either give kind of the
credentials to the Git repos,

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:13.850
and every single Git repo
for every single Kubernetes

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.345
cluster, and we push
those configs down,

00:15:16.345 --> 00:15:17.720
so the credentials
kind of remain

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.750
outside of it, or in this case,
we are giving those credentials

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:23.180
as a service account
to the GitLab runner,

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:25.970
and then giving the GitLab
runner the Git credentials,

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:27.650
so it can pull those manifests.

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:30.680
In order to do that, we have
to give this GitLab runner

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:33.013
access to pull those images.

00:15:33.013 --> 00:15:34.430
So I'm going to
click on this link

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.510
and I'm just going to
create some credentials.

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:40.050
So this is very similar to
just creating a Docker login.

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.050
And in this case, the
only thing it really needs

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:45.620
is read access to the registry,
so it can pull the images.

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:48.150
So I'll create a user
called git-reader.

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:49.760
It doesn't matter
what we call it.

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.590
We'll give it
read_registry access.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.630
I'll create the deploy
token, and it'll give me

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:55.530
this username and password.

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:59.390
We'll copy this password,
go back to our command line,

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:02.420
give it the username, which is
git-reader, copy the password,

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:05.590
and let it kind of
finish the bootstrap.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:09.640
So let's take a look at what
actually got created in GitLab.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.250
So I'm going to go into
GitLab, go to Groups,

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:14.360
and go to Your groups, so
we can see all of them.

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.027
So the first thing
you notice is that we

00:16:16.027 --> 00:16:18.715
have this new group created
called hello-kubecon-2019.

00:16:18.715 --> 00:16:21.130
If we go into that group, we
have the two repositories.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.470
The bottom one is your
application repository.

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:25.210
That's the one for
your developers.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.790
The top one is your env, or
your environment repository.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:30.820
That's the one for your resource
manifests for Kubernetes.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:32.210
We'll inspect
those here shortly.

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:34.360
I'm going to go back
into the groups,

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.647
and I'm going to look at this
group called platform-admins.

00:16:37.647 --> 00:16:38.980
And this is an operator's group.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:41.890
There's quite a few
repositories kind of in here.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:44.180
So I'll highlight a few of them.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:46.610
The first one are these
templated repositories,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:49.360
so the golang-template and
the golang-template-env.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:52.830
Again, like I said, it kind
of gives our developers

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:55.082
a starting point of
something they can code,

00:16:55.082 --> 00:16:57.040
so you don't have to do
this every single time.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.490
So you can have other
templates for Java, Ruby, Go,

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:02.602
and what have you.

00:17:02.602 --> 00:17:04.810
We heard about Anthos Config
Management this morning.

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:07.660
And this is the
platform that sets up

00:17:07.660 --> 00:17:10.450
kind of the foundation, the
namespaces, the registries,

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:11.770
and stuff like that.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.790
Inside of this repository--
this is, by the way,

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:15.730
what the folder structure
looks like-- there's

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.109
a namespaces folder.

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:18.579
So we go into the
namespaces folder

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:20.950
and the managed-apps folder.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.325
You can see that we now have--
as part of that bootstrap

00:17:23.325 --> 00:17:24.700
that we did with
the CLI command,

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:27.369
we have a
hello-kubecon-2019 folder.

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:28.830
We'll go into that folder.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.610
And you'll notice
that, like I said,

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:32.900
it creates the namespace
for that application.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:34.390
It creates that registry secret.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:36.760
That's that Docker login
that we just created.

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:39.280
And it feeds that to all
of the GitLab runners

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:40.930
in each namespace
for each cluster.

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:43.420
So you see the three runners,
two for the production

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:46.420
clusters-- prod-central,
prod-east-- and the staging.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.490
So now each cluster
has pull permissions

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.952
to pull images
from that registry.

00:17:50.952 --> 00:17:52.660
And if we want to look
at what that looks

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:54.040
like inside of the cluster--

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:56.050
so again, I'm in
the Cloud Console.

00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:58.533
We're looking at the Kubernetes
Engine Workloads page.

00:17:58.533 --> 00:18:00.950
And I've already put a filter
for that namespace on there.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:03.520
And if we hit Refresh,
you'll notice that we

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:04.918
have the three GitLab runners.

00:18:04.918 --> 00:18:06.460
We also have something
else deployed.

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:08.510
And we'll talk
about that shortly.

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:11.502
But I really wanted you to
focus on the three runners that

00:18:11.502 --> 00:18:13.210
are scoped to that
namespace-- so they're

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:15.940
running within that
hello-kubecon-2019 namespace--

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:18.280
in each of the clusters--
the two production clusters

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:19.810
and the one staging cluster.

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:22.630
And their permissions are
scoped within that namespace.

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:25.840
They can't put anything
outside of that.

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:29.193
So let's go back to our
platform-admins group.

00:18:29.193 --> 00:18:30.610
Another thing I
wanted to show you

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.810
was the shared CI/CD repository.

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:35.350
So this is where all of the
CI/CD for every single language

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:36.340
will sit here.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.480
So in the CI
folder, for example,

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:41.000
you'll have all of the manifests
for different CI files.

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:43.250
So we have one for Go
here as an example.

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:44.750
And you can see,
it's fairly simple.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:47.350
It's doing a unit test,
which just go get, go test,

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:49.000
and then just a build stage.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:53.690
Again, this could be as
deep as you'd like it to be.

00:18:53.690 --> 00:18:55.750
And then the last thing
I want to mention here

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.508
are Kustomize bases.

00:18:57.508 --> 00:18:59.050
These are the shared
Kustomize bases,

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:01.780
like Vic talked about,
for every language.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:02.500
We have a folder.

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:04.000
So we can go into
the golang folder.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.710
And in this demo,
we're just doing

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:06.835
a deployment and a service.

00:19:06.835 --> 00:19:09.340
But you can have other
Kubernetes manifests,

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:10.790
and they don't
look scary at all.

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:14.170
They look just like a vanilla
Kubernetes deployment.

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:16.195
And things, like we can
change our environment,

00:19:16.195 --> 00:19:17.920
so which environment
they're going into-- dev,

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:18.640
stage, or prod.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:20.290
We can change the image
name, because the majority

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:22.550
of the time, you're not
changing the entire YAML.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:23.967
You're just changing
a few things.

00:19:23.967 --> 00:19:26.340
And that's where that
patch kind of comes in.

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.810
And to show you that, now
let's go ahead and inspect

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.460
our actual application group.

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:33.880
So I'm going to go back into
hello-kubecon-2019 group,

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:37.203
and we're going to start
with the application repo.

00:19:37.203 --> 00:19:38.620
So again, this is
the boilerplate.

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:41.260
This is the repository that gets
handed over to the developers.

00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:42.635
And you can see,
the first thing,

00:19:42.635 --> 00:19:44.660
we just gave them
some boilerplate code.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:46.840
This is just a simple
Hello World! app

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:50.213
that the developers can
start iterating upon.

00:19:50.213 --> 00:19:52.255
In the same repositories,
we have the Dockerfile,

00:19:52.255 --> 00:19:53.892
so this is what
builds the artifacts.

00:19:53.892 --> 00:19:56.350
We have Skaffold, so they can
start doing some developments

00:19:56.350 --> 00:19:59.680
and local dev testing
before they even push this

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:00.730
to this particular repo.

00:20:00.730 --> 00:20:03.040
We have this gitlab-ci,
which is their CI/CD file

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:04.660
for this particular repo.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:08.080
This pipeline gets kicked
off anytime somebody commits,

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:10.450
makes a commit to the master
branch of this application

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:11.017
repo.

00:20:11.017 --> 00:20:12.850
And I already showed
you the test and build.

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:16.370
And Steve's going to show you
how the deployment piece works.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:20.290
What's interesting about this
is, as part of the application

00:20:20.290 --> 00:20:22.620
repo, we also have the
Kubernetes manifest.

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.280
So the developers
not only get to see

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:26.410
what actually gets
deployed, they also

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.420
have influence over
how it does it.

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:31.600
So inside of that k8s folder,
we have the three folders

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:33.380
for the three environments.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:35.530
So for example, if we go
to the staging folder,

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:37.690
first thing is that
it's pulling that shared

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:40.820
Kustomize bases from that repo
that I showed you earlier.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:43.270
So as an operator, I
can go and make a change

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:45.280
to that shared base one time.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:47.680
If, let's say, I want to
change the logging level,

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.320
or if I want to add resource
limits, or something,

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:52.570
I'll just change it one time,
and in the next rollout,

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.710
this will get applied
across the board.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:20:56.920
And then here's the patch,
the deployment patch

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:00.320
that is being applied for
this particular thing.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:01.700
And if we just
look at that very,

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:03.670
very quickly, all
we're doing here

00:21:03.670 --> 00:21:05.920
is giving it the
ENVIRONMENT variable

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.100
and then giving it the
name of the image, which

00:21:09.100 --> 00:21:10.883
Steve will show you shortly.

00:21:10.883 --> 00:21:12.550
So as a developer, I
can actually go in,

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:14.740
and I can change
this patch to-- let's

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:16.885
say if I want more
CPU or something,

00:21:16.885 --> 00:21:18.260
or if I want to
change the image,

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:20.218
if I want to push a
different image to staging,

00:21:20.218 --> 00:21:21.140
I can do that here.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:23.990
So I still have that
control over staging.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:25.790
So now I mentioned that
every time a commit

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:28.670
is made to the master branch,
a pipeline is kicked off.

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:30.330
So here on the
left-hand side, I'm

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.632
going to go to CI/CD, Pipelines.

00:21:32.632 --> 00:21:34.340
And you can see that
one pipeline because

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:36.173
of the initial bootstrapping
was kicked off.

00:21:36.173 --> 00:21:37.580
And let's click
on that pipeline.

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:39.800
And it's just a simple
four-stage CI/CD step.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:41.725
Again, this could be as
complex as you'd like.

00:21:41.725 --> 00:21:43.475
The first two are
pretty self-explanatory.

00:21:43.475 --> 00:21:45.230
And I already kind
of showed you those.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:47.300
This is the unit
test that happened,

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:49.070
and then the building
of that image.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:50.998
Once that image is
built in the registry,

00:21:50.998 --> 00:21:52.290
somehow we need to deploy that.

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:54.350
And for that, I'm going
to hand it off to Steve,

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:57.350
and he's going to talk more
about how that gets deployed.

00:21:57.350 --> 00:21:58.010
STEVE MCGHEE: Thank you, Ameer.

00:21:58.010 --> 00:21:58.746
AMEER ABBAS: Sure.

00:21:58.746 --> 00:22:00.246
STEVE MCGHEE:
[INAUDIBLE] all right?

00:22:00.246 --> 00:22:01.080
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:01.470
STEVE MCGHEE: Yeah?

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:01.920
All right, cool.

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:02.670
I'm Steve McGhee.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:03.690
I'm a former SRE.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.420
So I get the honor/duty
of pushing this thing

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.130
into production.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:10.230
So pray.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.310
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:22:11.310 --> 00:22:13.393
STEVE MCGHEE: So the first
thing we're going to do

00:22:13.393 --> 00:22:14.810
is it's still part
of the CI step,

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.601
but it's kind of the
beginning of CD, which

00:22:16.601 --> 00:22:17.920
is hydrating these manifests.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.560
So these are these YAML files
that we talked about before.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:22.973
So before we had the base
image and the patches,

00:22:22.973 --> 00:22:25.140
and you kind of think about
variables and templates.

00:22:25.140 --> 00:22:26.670
This is all of it expanded out.

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:28.270
So we have two files.

00:22:28.270 --> 00:22:30.750
One's going to be
Kustomize staging.

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:32.675
The other one's Kustomize prod.

00:22:32.675 --> 00:22:34.800
And that way, if we have
any trouble down the line,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:38.100
we know exactly what got
pushed to production.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:39.683
The first step is
just to build those.

00:22:39.683 --> 00:22:40.933
And we can click through here.

00:22:40.933 --> 00:22:42.660
And we can show you,
it builds them all.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:43.858
It's very simple.

00:22:43.858 --> 00:22:44.900
It builds one right here.

00:22:44.900 --> 00:22:47.520
It runs a Kustomize command
for Kustomize staging,

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:49.800
and then it does it
again for production.

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:51.730
So after that, we
have these two files.

00:22:51.730 --> 00:22:52.690
What we do with them?

00:22:52.690 --> 00:22:54.480
We just push them
over to another repo.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:56.130
So now we're moving
from the code repo

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:57.228
to the environment repo.

00:22:57.228 --> 00:22:59.520
I won't really show you this,
because it's just a copy.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:00.820
It's not very interesting.

00:23:00.820 --> 00:23:02.400
So let's back out
into the group.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:05.670
And we're going to show you the
env repo, the environment repo.

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:08.080
And we'll show you where
those files kind of end up.

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:09.150
So I go here.

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:09.760
Oh, my gosh!

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:10.380
Where are the files?

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:11.047
Well, we forgot.

00:23:11.047 --> 00:23:12.780
We actually pushed
it to a branch.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:14.575
We pushed it to
the staging branch.

00:23:14.575 --> 00:23:16.450
So as soon as we move
over there, here we go,

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:17.573
we've got our two files.

00:23:17.573 --> 00:23:18.490
So we can look at one.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:19.650
Let's look at staging.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:22.320
And it shouldn't be
scary in any way.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:23.610
It's pretty basic.

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.410
So we have our
environment label,

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.028
staging, just like we expect.

00:23:27.028 --> 00:23:28.320
It has the name, hello-kubecon.

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:30.403
So all those things that
looked all vanilla before

00:23:30.403 --> 00:23:32.700
have all been filled in
with the right stuff.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.180
So we saw before that there
were three runners running

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:36.995
in Kubernetes.

00:23:36.995 --> 00:23:38.370
And there was also
a job running.

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:41.368
There was the
actual staging app.

00:23:41.368 --> 00:23:42.660
So this is this guy right here.

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:43.830
It already got out there.

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:45.220
How the heck did that happen?

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:46.710
And the reason why
that happened is

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:49.530
because when we made
the first commit,

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:53.580
we pushed this file all the
way to the environment branch,

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.580
into the staging branch, and
our friend, the pipeline,

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:57.150
ran again.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:58.817
So let's see this new
pipeline that ran.

00:23:58.817 --> 00:24:00.180
This is a different pipeline.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:03.660
This one, doo-doo-doo,
just did one thing.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:05.210
It deployed to staging.

00:24:05.210 --> 00:24:06.210
So we can click in here.

00:24:06.210 --> 00:24:10.320
We can see there's this job
that ran, this binary that runs.

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:12.300
We're using something
called gke-deploy.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.952
And think of it just
as, like, kubectl apply,

00:24:14.952 --> 00:24:17.160
except it does a couple of
other little fancy things,

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:18.390
and it waits for it to succeed.

00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:19.765
And then once it
does, it sort of

00:24:19.765 --> 00:24:22.500
tells you, here, go look at your
job, it's running, it's great.

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.900
So we can see, yes,
indeed, it's running.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:26.820
So at this point, the
developer says, great.

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.110
My job is what I expected.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:29.880
The feature is
doing the featuring.

00:24:29.880 --> 00:24:30.810
Let's push it to prod.

00:24:30.810 --> 00:24:33.160
And then the SRE shows
up, and goes, you got it.

00:24:33.160 --> 00:24:34.410
Let's do it.

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:35.520
The schedule is correct.

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.780
We're ready to go.

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:40.360
And then, if you recall from the
kind of interesting flow chart,

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:41.820
the way we do this
is we're going

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:47.060
to do it by promoting
the environment

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.980
YAML from the staging
branch to the master branch.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:51.360
We do that in
GitLab using what's

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:53.760
called a merge request, which
you probably already know.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:55.770
We're going to make
a new merge request.

00:24:55.770 --> 00:24:58.620
We're going to give it a
very, very descriptive name,

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:01.430
like, go.

00:25:01.430 --> 00:25:03.110
Oh, no.

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:04.830
The computer
stopped computering.

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:06.330
OK.

00:25:06.330 --> 00:25:07.330
Prod.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:08.162
OK.

00:25:08.162 --> 00:25:09.370
So we're going to go to prod.

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:10.600
So we want to make
sure we're actually

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:12.010
going from staging to master.

00:25:12.010 --> 00:25:13.340
That looks pretty good.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:15.160
We're going to submit
a merge request.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:16.510
Some other person might come in.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:18.090
Like, you might want to
have another set of eyes

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:19.180
go, yeah, this looks fine.

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:20.635
They might come
in and say, well,

00:25:20.635 --> 00:25:22.010
let's take a look
at the changes,

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:23.817
make sure they're
exactly what we expect.

00:25:23.817 --> 00:25:25.900
You might also have other
things in your pipeline,

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:29.837
like, to make sure that we have
the right amount of resources

00:25:29.837 --> 00:25:30.670
in place, et cetera.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.360
This is really boring, because
it's just like a blank entry.

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:34.610
But let's say it looks good.

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:35.110
We're going to merge it.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:35.950
Let's go.

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:38.710
So now what happens
in this process?

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.108
Yet another pipeline happens.

00:25:40.108 --> 00:25:40.900
Of course, it does.

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:42.370
That's how this works.

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:45.428
In this case, there is
one step for each cluster,

00:25:45.428 --> 00:25:46.720
so for each production cluster.

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:49.345
And it's exactly the same as the
staging one, except this time,

00:25:49.345 --> 00:25:50.990
it's pushing to production.

00:25:50.990 --> 00:25:52.790
So we have two clusters here.

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:54.127
One is just in central1.

00:25:54.127 --> 00:25:55.210
Look, it already finished.

00:25:55.210 --> 00:25:56.650
And the next one's
going to be in east1.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.650
You might have another
step in here that's like,

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:02.260
wait, or check the canary,
or something-something,

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.100
but we just did
something very simple.

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:06.190
So let's take a
look at Kubernetes

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:07.480
and make sure it's working.

00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:10.255
It should be ready.

00:26:10.255 --> 00:26:12.150
Ah, it's working!

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:14.450
So we have one
running in central1.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.590
And then the us-east1
is coming up as well.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:16.610
So

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:18.152
Let's take a look
at the Logs Viewer.

00:26:18.152 --> 00:26:21.060
And we're going to see
if it is really running.

00:26:21.060 --> 00:26:23.330
So for each cluster,
we should be

00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:25.010
able to see the
health checks come by.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:26.510
And ideally, what
we're going to see

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:31.070
is three sets of apps all
running kind of skewed

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:32.990
from each other.

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:33.530
Stream.

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:34.490
Stream.

00:26:34.490 --> 00:26:36.560
This is it, guys.

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:38.561
This is going to be it.

00:26:38.561 --> 00:26:45.390
And this is where
we pray to Kelsey.

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:50.090
So we have health
checks in staging.

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:51.260
We have it in prod.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.010
We have it in prod-central.

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:55.607
And eventually, we'll have
ones from prod-east as well.

00:26:55.607 --> 00:26:56.190
But that's it.

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:57.480
Our code is deployed.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:58.410
It's in many places.

00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.440
Another thing to point out,
prod-east and prod-central

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:02.150
could be two different
types of Kubernetes

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:03.290
on two different platforms.

00:27:03.290 --> 00:27:05.090
This is the amazing
thing about Anthos.

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:05.850
It doesn't care.

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:06.725
It's just Kubernetes.

00:27:06.725 --> 00:27:07.840
So that's it.

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:08.445
[CHEERING]

00:27:08.445 --> 00:27:09.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:11.020
VIC IGLESIAS: Woo!

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:11.520
All right.

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:14.830
If we could go back to
slides for a little bit.

00:27:14.830 --> 00:27:15.720
That was pretty good.

00:27:15.720 --> 00:27:19.610
I will continue to bring them
along for future Anthos Days.

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:21.530
I don't want to do the demos.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:22.940
So let's take a quick review.

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:24.030
We saw a lot of things.

00:27:24.030 --> 00:27:26.762
A lot of terms flew
around-- code and arrows

00:27:26.762 --> 00:27:27.720
and all kinds of stuff.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.710
So we had developers committing
to an application repo.

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:31.867
That was kicking off CI.

00:27:31.867 --> 00:27:33.950
And then that built an
artifact, our Docker image,

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:35.990
and stored it in our
container registry.

00:27:35.990 --> 00:27:39.470
From there, it was added to
our application configuration.

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:42.200
The operators were able to
get their base configuration,

00:27:42.200 --> 00:27:43.580
and put that into
the application

00:27:43.580 --> 00:27:45.693
configuration with that image.

00:27:45.693 --> 00:27:48.110
And then that was committed
to our environment repo, where

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:50.288
the CD process was started.

00:27:50.288 --> 00:27:52.580
The other thing that we saw
is that policy and security

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:54.650
engineers were able
to create that landing

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:57.470
zone for our applications
and those namespaces

00:27:57.470 --> 00:28:00.920
with the right policies
in a Git repo as well,

00:28:00.920 --> 00:28:03.870
and have those propagate
to all our clusters.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:06.050
So that's kind of
the shape of what

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:11.270
a platform might look like if
it's built on top of Anthos.

00:28:11.270 --> 00:28:12.800
The last thing I
want to just review

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:17.540
is a lot about how we propagated
our best practices as operators

00:28:17.540 --> 00:28:18.920
through this platform.

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:23.300
With the ability to
use GitLabs includes

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:24.890
we can have a
centralized repo, where

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:27.500
we're having most
of our CI steps

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:29.832
that as a library that
developers can pick from.

00:28:29.832 --> 00:28:31.290
So they have a good
starting point,

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:32.930
and then can iterate from there.

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:35.720
We also can do the same thing
for our continuous delivery

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:36.572
processes.

00:28:36.572 --> 00:28:38.030
And with our
application config, we

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:40.640
have our shared Kustomize
bases, which we can store in Git

00:28:40.640 --> 00:28:43.108
and then reference from
our application repos.

00:28:43.108 --> 00:28:44.900
And so we're pulling
in our best practices.

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:47.780
And then as we iterate
on those as operators,

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:51.110
those automatically propagate on
the next run to our developers.

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:55.670
Also, we have the ability
to store our environment

00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:58.220
configuration in a
repository, and have

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:00.110
that reviewed by
SRE and security

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:02.270
and whoever else
before any changes are

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:05.160
merged into production.

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.260
So with that, I'd ask
you to please come talk

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.260
to us at either the Google
Cloud booth or here--

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:11.400
I think we have a break
coming up right now--

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:13.810
and ask about the
Anthos platform demo.

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:14.910
Thank you, folks, so much.

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:17.360
[APPLAUSE]

